window.docContents[128].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems":{"contents":"Energy arguments produce solutions without explicit formulas. Green's first identity\non a coordinate box already shows the mechanism: for the difference  of two\n solutions with the same boundary values, the identity collapses to\n, and uniqueness follows without any representation\nformula. The weak theory keeps that argument and drops the classical\nregularity. On  the Dirichlet form\n is bounded, the Poincaré inequality of the\nSobolev chapter makes it coercive on bounded domains, and Lax–Milgram returns a\nunique weak solution of  for every , together\nwith an a priori estimate, continuous dependence on the load, and a\ncharacterization as the minimizer of the Dirichlet energy. For classical\ncompetitors on a bounded domain with  graph boundary, the\nclassical Dirichlet principle\ngives the same characterization.\n\nThe same pattern extends to variable principal coefficients, where\nalmost-everywhere ellipticity yields the integrated coercivity, and to\nlower-order terms, where coercivity can fail and a Gårding inequality with a\nspectral shift takes its place. The modified Helmholtz problem\n with  works on any measurable domain:\nthe gradient and mass terms together control the full  norm without a\nPoincaré inequality. The shifted\nlower-order solver is the input of the Fredholm section, and the weak solutions\nbuilt here are what the regularity section improves.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations","header":"5.4. Energy and Weak Boundary-Value Problems","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems"}});